.TH MKFS.FILSYS 1 "August 26, 2026" "filsys 1.2.2" "File Format and Disk Utilities"
.SH NAME
mkfs.filsys \- construct a Research Unix filesystem in a disk image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkfs.filsys
.RB [ \-v
.IR <v0|v1|v2|v3|v4|v5|v6|v7|32v> ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR block ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR boot ]
.I image
.RI [ blocks ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkfs.filsys
constructs a Research Unix filesystem by writing on the image file
.IR image .
It builds a fresh superblock, a zeroed i-list, an interleaved free-block
list, and an empty root directory, and grows the file to the filesystem
size if needed.  The image is opened read-write and created if it does not
already exist.
.PP
The edition of the filesystem is selected with
.BR \-v :
v0 selects the word-addressed PDP-7 filesystem; v1, v2, or v3 select the First
through Third Edition (one format, bitmap-allocated); v4, v5, or v6 select the
Sixth Edition format (V4 and V5
are byte-identical to V6 on disk); v7 selects Seventh Edition; 32v selects 32V
(V7 for the VAX, little-endian).  The default is
v7.  V6 and V7 differ
on disk: V6 has 16 32-byte inodes per block, 16-bit block numbers, and an
i-list whose size is stored as a block count; V7 has 8 64-byte inodes per
block, 24-bit block numbers, and an i-list whose size is stored as the first
data block.
.PP
A Research Unix disk image holds one or more filesystems
.RI ( partitions ),
selected by a partition table that is compiled into the kernel, not stored on
the disk.  By default the filesystem is written at block 0 (the first
partition); the
.B \-o
option places it at a different block offset within the image.  Block 0 of
each filesystem is the boot block; block 1 is the superblock; blocks 2 onward
are the i-list.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-v " <v0|v1|v2|v3|v4|v5|v6|v7|32v>"
Filesystem edition.  v0 is the word-addressed PDP-7; v1, v2 and v3 are one
format (First through Third Edition); v4, v5 and v6 are byte-identical (the V6
format); v7 is V7.
Default v7.  A bare number
.RB ( 0 ,
.BR 1 ,
.BR 4 ,
\&...) is also accepted.
.TP
.BI \-o " block"
Place the filesystem at
.I block
(512-byte blocks) within the image, instead of block 0.
.TP
.BI \-b " boot"
Write
.I boot
(a PDP-11
.BR a.out (5)
program, V7 magic 0407) as block 0 of the filesystem, before the superblock
at block 1.  This is the standard PDP-11 boot block: text plus data, at most
one block (512 bytes).  Without
.BR \-b ,
block 0 is left untouched.
.TP
.I blocks
Size of the filesystem in blocks.  If omitted, the size defaults to the image's
own size (from block 0, or from
.IR \-o ),
with a minimum of a small usable volume.
.SH BOOT BLOCKS
filsys ships two PDP-11 block-0 bootstraps, assembled from the Seventh Edition
.I /usr/mdec
sources and installed into the package data directory
.RB ( share/filsys
under the install prefix):
.TP
.B hpuboot
the RP06 (HP controller) disk bootstrap.
.TP
.B rpuboot
the RP03/04/05/06 disk bootstrap.
.PP
Pass one to
.B \-b
to write it into block 0 of a new filesystem, e.g.
.PP
.RS
.nf
mkfs.filsys -b /usr/local/share/filsys/hpuboot rp06-0.disk
.fi
.RE
.SH HISTORICAL FORM
The Seventh Edition
.I mkfs
was invoked as
.B "/etc/mkfs special proto"
and read a
.I prototype
file whose first token named a file to be copied onto block zero as the
bootstrap program, whose second token was the filesystem size, whose third
token was the i-list size, and whose remaining tokens described the root
directory and its contents.  The V6/V7
.I mkfs.filsys
retains the notion of a boot block at block zero (now the
.B \-b
option) but drops the prototype file in favour of an empty root and an
explicit
.I blocks
argument; the i-list is sized automatically.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mkfs.filsys image.dk             # size the fs to the whole image (V7)
mkfs.filsys -v 6 image.dk        # ...as a V6 (V4/V5) filesystem
mkfs.filsys -o 18392 image.dk    # start the fs at block 18392 (a partition)
mkfs.filsys -b /v7/mdec/rp06boot image.dk   # write a PDP-11 boot block
mkfs.filsys image.dk 5000        # an explicit 5000-block filesystem
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsck.filsys (1),
.BR findfs.filsys (1),
.BR mount.filsys (1),
.BR filsys (5),
.BR mkfs (8)
